{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-C.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"item\": 1,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA launch the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — NVIDIA launches the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\",\n   \"rung 2: No — NVIDIA has not launched the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber NVL144 has slipped from its planned 2027 launch to 2028, citing PCB manufacturing challenges; that makes a launch after the 2027-12-31 deadline the more likely outcome and crosses the binary rung boundary. NVIDIA’s “Our roadmap is intact” is a counterweight, but it gives no specific launch timing or operational detail. The broader plan to scale interconnected systems to as many as 576 GPUs by 2027 is not specific confirmation of a Kyber NVL144 launch.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 2,\n  \"question\": \"What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: below 800,000,000,000 USD — below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD — from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD — from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\\")\",\n   \"rung 4: from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD — from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more — 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion, while its 19.5 GW capacity estimate does not translate into a specific capex total. Project delays and execution constraints are counterweights, but are not quantified as reductions to the four hyperscalers’ 2027 spend. Broader AI-capex forecasts and supplier activity do not establish a higher four-company, 2027 data-center-capex figure.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"article_title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 3,\n  \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Lowest range — Combined spending is below $800 billion.\",\n   \"rung 2: Lower-middle range — Combined spending is at least $800 billion but below $900 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection — Combined spending is at least $900 billion but below $1.1 trillion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Upper-middle range — Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion but below $1.2 trillion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Highest range — Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"article_title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 4,\n  \"question\": \"What total amount in USD of credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments will NVIDIA have signed for AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model by 2027-12-31, as established by NVIDIA’s filings or signed partner agreements?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Strongly bullish — below 100,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: Bullish — from 100,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral — from 1,000,000,000 USD to below 5,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\",\n   \"rung 4: Bearish — from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 10,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: Strongly bearish — 10,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is no stated market, company, or consensus amount, which sits in rung 3 ($1B to below $5B). The implied total remains about $15.25B: Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88B backstop plus an estimated $10.37B for Firmus, scaling Sharon’s amount by GPU count to half of Firmus’s 170,000-GPU target because the evidence does not establish that the full target volume is in hand. Firmus’s reported $25–30B of committed off-take supports the project’s demand, but it is not itself NVIDIA credit support and does not establish a full-volume guarantee amount. The estimate remains above rung 5’s $10B boundary. The reported CoreWeave backstop is a precedent outside the new model and is not included.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 5,\n  \"question\": \"Will China's government or a regulator permit NVIDIA to sell H200 AI chips to Chinese customers by 2027-01-31, as shown by an official Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — By 2027-01-31, an official Chinese government or regulator decision permits H200 sales to Chinese customers, or NVIDIA confirms H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers.\",\n   \"rung 2: Ruled out — The Chinese government or a Chinese regulator formally denies permission for H200 sales to Chinese customers or formally prohibits those sales. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n   \"rung 3: Still open — Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; as a market/company reference for this question, that corresponds to rung 3, Still open, rather than a qualifying Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed sale by the deadline. The forecast differs because a specific report says Chinese officials told named firms they would be permitted to buy limited H200 quantities, and a U.S. trade official later said very few H200 shipments to China and Hong Kong had taken place. Those reports do not themselves satisfy the thesis's evidence test, but together make a qualifying Chinese decision or NVIDIA confirmation by 2027-01-31 more likely than the event remaining open. The reports of slow import reviews and domestic-chip preferences temper that forecast; no formal Chinese denial or abandonment supports rung 2.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 6,\n  \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Lowest range — Combined spending is below $800 billion.\",\n   \"rung 2: Lower-middle range — Combined spending is at least $800 billion but below $900 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection — Combined spending is at least $900 billion but below $1.1 trillion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Upper-middle range — Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion but below $1.2 trillion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Highest range — Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 7,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first shipments of its Vera Rubin AI chip platform to customers have begun by December 31, 2026, through a press release, earnings call, or quarterly filing?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — NVIDIA confirms that first shipments of Vera Rubin to customers have begun by December 31, 2026.\",\n   \"rung 2: Ruled out — NVIDIA formally announces after July 2, 2026, that it has canceled or abandoned the Vera Rubin platform before any first shipment to a customer. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n   \"rung 3: Still open — Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's reported plan for Rubin systems to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, which sits at rung 1. The full-production report, the Q3/fall first-shipment schedule, and the later report that the architecture remains on track for late 2026 make NVIDIA confirmation by year-end the most likely outcome. Reports of delays are either unattributed or concern Rubin Ultra/Kyber; NVIDIA disputed the delay claims, and the clarification says the 2026 Rubin GPUs using the Oberon rack are not part of that reported delay. There is no evidence of a post-July 2 decision abandoning the Vera Rubin platform as a whole, so rung 2 is not supported. The forecast is that shipments and confirmation occur by the deadline, not that they have already been established.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 8,\n  \"question\": \"What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: below 800,000,000,000 USD — below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD — from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD — from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\\")\",\n   \"rung 4: from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD — from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more — 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion, while its 19.5 GW capacity estimate does not translate into a specific capex total. Project delays and execution constraints are counterweights, but are not quantified as reductions to the four hyperscalers’ 2027 spend. Broader AI-capex forecasts and supplier activity do not establish a higher four-company, 2027 data-center-capex figure.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 9,\n  \"question\": \"How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: below 500,000,000,000 USD — below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD — from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD — from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year\\\")\",\n   \"rung 4: from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD — from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: 800,000,000,000 USD or more — 800,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies the change to the group's data-center-only spending. Delayed projects and execution constraints lean the other way, but do not quantify a reduction in the big four's 2026 spend. The evidence therefore leaves the most likely total within rung 3; it does not support crossing either the $600 billion or $700 billion boundary.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"article_title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 10,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first shipments of its Vera Rubin AI chip platform to customers have begun by December 31, 2026, through a press release, earnings call, or quarterly filing?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — NVIDIA confirms that first shipments of Vera Rubin to customers have begun by December 31, 2026.\",\n   \"rung 2: Ruled out — NVIDIA formally announces after July 2, 2026, that it has canceled or abandoned the Vera Rubin platform before any first shipment to a customer. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n   \"rung 3: Still open — Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's reported plan for Rubin systems to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, which sits at rung 1. The full-production report, the Q3/fall first-shipment schedule, and the later report that the architecture remains on track for late 2026 make NVIDIA confirmation by year-end the most likely outcome. Reports of delays are either unattributed or concern Rubin Ultra/Kyber; NVIDIA disputed the delay claims, and the clarification says the 2026 Rubin GPUs using the Oberon rack are not part of that reported delay. There is no evidence of a post-July 2 decision abandoning the Vera Rubin platform as a whole, so rung 2 is not supported. The forecast is that shipments and confirmation occur by the deadline, not that they have already been established.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 11,\n  \"question\": \"What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: below 800,000,000,000 USD — below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD — from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD — from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\\")\",\n   \"rung 4: from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD — from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more — 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion, while its 19.5 GW capacity estimate does not translate into a specific capex total. Project delays and execution constraints are counterweights, but are not quantified as reductions to the four hyperscalers’ 2027 spend. Broader AI-capex forecasts and supplier activity do not establish a higher four-company, 2027 data-center-capex figure.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 12,\n  \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Lowest range — Combined spending is below $800 billion.\",\n   \"rung 2: Lower-middle range — Combined spending is at least $800 billion but below $900 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection — Combined spending is at least $900 billion but below $1.1 trillion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Upper-middle range — Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion but below $1.2 trillion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Highest range — Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 13,\n  \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud programme by its next annual report?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Strongly bullish — NVIDIA reports no commitments under the programme.\",\n   \"rung 2: Bullish — Commitments total more than $0 and less than $1 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral — Commitments total at least $1 billion and less than $5 billion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Bearish — Commitments total at least $5 billion and less than $10 billion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Strongly bearish — Commitments total at least $10 billion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s positive but unquantified announcement of credit support, which sits at rung 2 under the ladder convention. The implied amount is about $9 billion by the next annual report: Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88 billion backstop, plus an estimated roughly $4.1 billion for Firmus. That Firmus estimate applies Sharon’s approximately $122,000 per-GPU backstop value to 170,000 GPUs, then discounts the result to about one-fifth because Firmus’s deployment is scheduled in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 and the report is due in February 2027. The estimate is above rung 3’s $5 billion boundary but below rung 5’s $10 billion boundary. The whole-project, undiscounted extrapolation would be much higher, but the evidence does not establish that Firmus’s full planned volume will be committed by the due date.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"neither Nvidia or the Neoclouds have any intention of using the backstop\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 14,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: No — NVIDIA has not signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\",\n   \"rung 2: Yes — NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The evidence makes a signed guarantee by the due date more likely than not, but does not establish that it has been signed. The specific report of negotiations for OpenAI’s Ohio lease is supported directionally by NVIDIA’s subsequently announced credit-support model and evidence that Firmus and Sharon AI adopted it; that precedent is not itself an Ohio-lease guarantee. The general AI-capacity and capex themes add only contextual support.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"article_title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 15,\n  \"question\": \"How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: below 500,000,000,000 USD — below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD — from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD — from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year\\\")\",\n   \"rung 4: from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD — from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: 800,000,000,000 USD or more — 800,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies the change to the group's data-center-only spending. Delayed projects and execution constraints lean the other way, but do not quantify a reduction in the big four's 2026 spend. The evidence therefore leaves the most likely total within rung 3; it does not support crossing either the $600 billion or $700 billion boundary.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"tight market conditions could last until 2028\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 16,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA launch the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — NVIDIA launches the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\",\n   \"rung 2: No — NVIDIA has not launched the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber NVL144 has slipped from its planned 2027 launch to 2028, citing PCB manufacturing challenges; that makes a launch after the 2027-12-31 deadline the more likely outcome and crosses the binary rung boundary. NVIDIA’s “Our roadmap is intact” is a counterweight, but it gives no specific launch timing or operational detail. The broader plan to scale interconnected systems to as many as 576 GPUs by 2027 is not specific confirmation of a Kyber NVL144 launch.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 17,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first shipments of its Vera Rubin AI chip platform to customers have begun by December 31, 2026, through a press release, earnings call, or quarterly filing?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — NVIDIA confirms that first shipments of Vera Rubin to customers have begun by December 31, 2026.\",\n   \"rung 2: Ruled out — NVIDIA formally announces after July 2, 2026, that it has canceled or abandoned the Vera Rubin platform before any first shipment to a customer. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n   \"rung 3: Still open — Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's reported plan for Rubin systems to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, which sits at rung 1. The full-production report, the Q3/fall first-shipment schedule, and the later report that the architecture remains on track for late 2026 make NVIDIA confirmation by year-end the most likely outcome. Reports of delays are either unattributed or concern Rubin Ultra/Kyber; NVIDIA disputed the delay claims, and the clarification says the 2026 Rubin GPUs using the Oberon rack are not part of that reported delay. There is no evidence of a post-July 2 decision abandoning the Vera Rubin platform as a whole, so rung 2 is not supported. The forecast is that shipments and confirmation occur by the deadline, not that they have already been established.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 18,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA launch the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — NVIDIA launches the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\",\n   \"rung 2: No — NVIDIA has not launched the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber NVL144 has slipped from its planned 2027 launch to 2028, citing PCB manufacturing challenges; that makes a launch after the 2027-12-31 deadline the more likely outcome and crosses the binary rung boundary. NVIDIA’s “Our roadmap is intact” is a counterweight, but it gives no specific launch timing or operational detail. The broader plan to scale interconnected systems to as many as 576 GPUs by 2027 is not specific confirmation of a Kyber NVL144 launch.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 19,\n  \"question\": \"What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: below 800,000,000,000 USD — below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD — from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD — from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\\")\",\n   \"rung 4: from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD — from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more — 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion, while its 19.5 GW capacity estimate does not translate into a specific capex total. Project delays and execution constraints are counterweights, but are not quantified as reductions to the four hyperscalers’ 2027 spend. Broader AI-capex forecasts and supplier activity do not establish a higher four-company, 2027 data-center-capex figure.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"article_title\": \"DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 20,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA launch the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — NVIDIA launches the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\",\n   \"rung 2: No — NVIDIA has not launched the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber NVL144 has slipped from its planned 2027 launch to 2028, citing PCB manufacturing challenges; that makes a launch after the 2027-12-31 deadline the more likely outcome and crosses the binary rung boundary. NVIDIA’s “Our roadmap is intact” is a counterweight, but it gives no specific launch timing or operational detail. The broader plan to scale interconnected systems to as many as 576 GPUs by 2027 is not specific confirmation of a Kyber NVL144 launch.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 21,\n  \"question\": \"What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: below 800,000,000,000 USD — below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD — from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD — from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\\")\",\n   \"rung 4: from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD — from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more — 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion, while its 19.5 GW capacity estimate does not translate into a specific capex total. Project delays and execution constraints are counterweights, but are not quantified as reductions to the four hyperscalers’ 2027 spend. Broader AI-capex forecasts and supplier activity do not establish a higher four-company, 2027 data-center-capex figure.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"neither Nvidia or the Neoclouds have any intention of using the backstop\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 22,\n  \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud programme by its next annual report?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Strongly bullish — NVIDIA reports no commitments under the programme.\",\n   \"rung 2: Bullish — Commitments total more than $0 and less than $1 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral — Commitments total at least $1 billion and less than $5 billion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Bearish — Commitments total at least $5 billion and less than $10 billion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Strongly bearish — Commitments total at least $10 billion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s positive but unquantified announcement of credit support, which sits at rung 2 under the ladder convention. The implied amount is about $9 billion by the next annual report: Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88 billion backstop, plus an estimated roughly $4.1 billion for Firmus. That Firmus estimate applies Sharon’s approximately $122,000 per-GPU backstop value to 170,000 GPUs, then discounts the result to about one-fifth because Firmus’s deployment is scheduled in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 and the report is due in February 2027. The estimate is above rung 3’s $5 billion boundary but below rung 5’s $10 billion boundary. The whole-project, undiscounted extrapolation would be much higher, but the evidence does not establish that Firmus’s full planned volume will be committed by the due date.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 23,\n  \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud programme by its next annual report?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Strongly bullish — NVIDIA reports no commitments under the programme.\",\n   \"rung 2: Bullish — Commitments total more than $0 and less than $1 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral — Commitments total at least $1 billion and less than $5 billion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Bearish — Commitments total at least $5 billion and less than $10 billion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Strongly bearish — Commitments total at least $10 billion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s positive but unquantified announcement of credit support, which sits at rung 2 under the ladder convention. The implied amount is about $9 billion by the next annual report: Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88 billion backstop, plus an estimated roughly $4.1 billion for Firmus. That Firmus estimate applies Sharon’s approximately $122,000 per-GPU backstop value to 170,000 GPUs, then discounts the result to about one-fifth because Firmus’s deployment is scheduled in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 and the report is due in February 2027. The estimate is above rung 3’s $5 billion boundary but below rung 5’s $10 billion boundary. The whole-project, undiscounted extrapolation would be much higher, but the evidence does not establish that Firmus’s full planned volume will be committed by the due date.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"article_title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 24,\n  \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Lowest range — Combined spending is below $800 billion.\",\n   \"rung 2: Lower-middle range — Combined spending is at least $800 billion but below $900 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection — Combined spending is at least $900 billion but below $1.1 trillion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Upper-middle range — Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion but below $1.2 trillion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Highest range — Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Comments from Meta’s Mark Zuckerberg about slower-than-expected progress in AI agents\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n   \"article_title\": \"TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 25,\n  \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Lowest range — Combined spending is below $500 billion.\",\n   \"rung 2: Lower-middle range — Combined spending is at least $500 billion but below $600 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan — Combined spending is at least $600 billion but below $700 billion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Upper-middle range — Combined spending is at least $700 billion but below $800 billion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Highest range — Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data-center portion of the four-company total. The broader $765 billion AI-infrastructure estimate is not the same scope, and the general statements about rising spending do not quantify an adjustment. The implied total therefore remains below rung 4’s $700 billion boundary and above rung 2’s $600 billion boundary.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"article_title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 26,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: No — NVIDIA has not signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\",\n   \"rung 2: Yes — NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The evidence makes a signed guarantee by the due date more likely than not, but does not establish that it has been signed. The specific report of negotiations for OpenAI’s Ohio lease is supported directionally by NVIDIA’s subsequently announced credit-support model and evidence that Firmus and Sharon AI adopted it; that precedent is not itself an Ohio-lease guarantee. The general AI-capacity and capex themes add only contextual support.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"article_title\": \"NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 27,\n  \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Lowest range — Combined spending is below $500 billion.\",\n   \"rung 2: Lower-middle range — Combined spending is at least $500 billion but below $600 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan — Combined spending is at least $600 billion but below $700 billion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Upper-middle range — Combined spending is at least $700 billion but below $800 billion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Highest range — Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data-center portion of the four-company total. The broader $765 billion AI-infrastructure estimate is not the same scope, and the general statements about rising spending do not quantify an adjustment. The implied total therefore remains below rung 4’s $700 billion boundary and above rung 2’s $600 billion boundary.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 28,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: No — NVIDIA has not signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\",\n   \"rung 2: Yes — NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The evidence makes a signed guarantee by the due date more likely than not, but does not establish that it has been signed. The specific report of negotiations for OpenAI’s Ohio lease is supported directionally by NVIDIA’s subsequently announced credit-support model and evidence that Firmus and Sharon AI adopted it; that precedent is not itself an Ohio-lease guarantee. The general AI-capacity and capex themes add only contextual support.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"article_title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 29,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: No — NVIDIA has not signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\",\n   \"rung 2: Yes — NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The evidence makes a signed guarantee by the due date more likely than not, but does not establish that it has been signed. The specific report of negotiations for OpenAI’s Ohio lease is supported directionally by NVIDIA’s subsequently announced credit-support model and evidence that Firmus and Sharon AI adopted it; that precedent is not itself an Ohio-lease guarantee. The general AI-capacity and capex themes add only contextual support.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 30,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: No — NVIDIA has not signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\",\n   \"rung 2: Yes — NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The evidence makes a signed guarantee by the due date more likely than not, but does not establish that it has been signed. The specific report of negotiations for OpenAI’s Ohio lease is supported directionally by NVIDIA’s subsequently announced credit-support model and evidence that Firmus and Sharon AI adopted it; that precedent is not itself an Ohio-lease guarantee. The general AI-capacity and capex themes add only contextual support.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 31,\n  \"question\": \"Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — China has authorized all three named firms to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31.\",\n   \"rung 2: No — China has not authorized all three named firms to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s, makes authorization of all three by the deadline more likely than continued non-authorization. This crosses the binary rung boundary, though the reports do not establish that authorization has already occurred. The reported refusal and the lack of a stated date or confirmed approvals temper, but do not outweigh, the six-month-plus window.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"article_title\": \"DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 32,\n  \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Lowest range — Combined spending is below $500 billion.\",\n   \"rung 2: Lower-middle range — Combined spending is at least $500 billion but below $600 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan — Combined spending is at least $600 billion but below $700 billion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Upper-middle range — Combined spending is at least $700 billion but below $800 billion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Highest range — Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data-center portion of the four-company total. The broader $765 billion AI-infrastructure estimate is not the same scope, and the general statements about rising spending do not quantify an adjustment. The implied total therefore remains below rung 4’s $700 billion boundary and above rung 2’s $600 billion boundary.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Comments from Meta’s Mark Zuckerberg about slower-than-expected progress in AI agents\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n   \"article_title\": \"TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 33,\n  \"question\": \"Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — China has authorized all three named firms to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31.\",\n   \"rung 2: No — China has not authorized all three named firms to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s, makes authorization of all three by the deadline more likely than continued non-authorization. This crosses the binary rung boundary, though the reports do not establish that authorization has already occurred. The reported refusal and the lack of a stated date or confirmed approvals temper, but do not outweigh, the six-month-plus window.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"article_title\": \"DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 34,\n  \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud programme by its next annual report?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Strongly bullish — NVIDIA reports no commitments under the programme.\",\n   \"rung 2: Bullish — Commitments total more than $0 and less than $1 billion.\",\n   \"rung 3: Neutral — Commitments total at least $1 billion and less than $5 billion.\",\n   \"rung 4: Bearish — Commitments total at least $5 billion and less than $10 billion.\",\n   \"rung 5: Strongly bearish — Commitments total at least $10 billion.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s positive but unquantified announcement of credit support, which sits at rung 2 under the ladder convention. The implied amount is about $9 billion by the next annual report: Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88 billion backstop, plus an estimated roughly $4.1 billion for Firmus. That Firmus estimate applies Sharon’s approximately $122,000 per-GPU backstop value to 170,000 GPUs, then discounts the result to about one-fifth because Firmus’s deployment is scheduled in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 and the report is due in February 2027. The estimate is above rung 3’s $5 billion boundary but below rung 5’s $10 billion boundary. The whole-project, undiscounted extrapolation would be much higher, but the evidence does not establish that Firmus’s full planned volume will be committed by the due date.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 35,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA launch the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: Yes — NVIDIA launches the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\",\n   \"rung 2: No — NVIDIA has not launched the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber NVL144 has slipped from its planned 2027 launch to 2028, citing PCB manufacturing challenges; that makes a launch after the 2027-12-31 deadline the more likely outcome and crosses the binary rung boundary. NVIDIA’s “Our roadmap is intact” is a counterweight, but it gives no specific launch timing or operational detail. The broader plan to scale interconnected systems to as many as 576 GPUs by 2027 is not specific confirmation of a Kyber NVL144 launch.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 36,\n  \"question\": \"How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: below 500,000,000,000 USD — below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 2: from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD — from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 3: from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD — from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year\\\")\",\n   \"rung 4: from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD — from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n   \"rung 5: 800,000,000,000 USD or more — 800,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies the change to the group's data-center-only spending. Delayed projects and execution constraints lean the other way, but do not quantify a reduction in the big four's 2026 spend. The evidence therefore leaves the most likely total within rung 3; it does not support crossing either the $600 billion or $700 billion boundary.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 37,\n  \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01?\",\n  \"ladder\": [\n   \"rung 1: No — NVIDIA has not signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\",\n   \"rung 2: Yes — NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\"\n  ],\n  \"judgement\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The evidence makes a signed guarantee by the due date more likely than not, but does not establish that it has been signed. The specific report of negotiations for OpenAI’s Ohio lease is supported directionally by NVIDIA’s subsequently announced credit-support model and evidence that Firmus and Sharon AI adopted it; that precedent is not itself an Ohio-lease guarantee. The general AI-capacity and capex themes add only contextual support.\"\n  },\n  \"fact\": {\n   \"passage\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion\",\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"article_title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\"\n  }\n }\n]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
